Trigger Finger
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Digit catching/locking, Palpable A1 pulley nodule, Painful passive extension release

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-062
Reason for Visit: Acute, agonizing locking of a finger in a fully flexed position, completely unresponsive to patient manipulation and causing severe distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient who awoke with a finger rigidly locked in a bent position; attempts to force it straight resulted in excruciating pain. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for an acute flexor tendon rupture or septic tenosynovitis, and to perform a safe, professionally guided manual release of the locked digit prior to daytime hand clinic services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I woke up and my ring finger is locked completely shut into my palm; when I try to pry it open, the pain is agonizing."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute, severe locking episode of the right ring finger. Timeline: Insidious history over the past 2 months of painless or mildly painful clicking, snapping, or catching of the digit during active flexion. Patient reports pronounced morning stiffness heavily localized to the affected finger. This morning, the patient awoke to find the affected digit rigidly locked in a fully flexed posture. Patient describes a terrifying, severe catching sensation. Previous mild locking episodes were self-managed, but today, attempting to actively extend the tightly flexed finger against resistance produces agonizing, sharp pain. Patient notes a dull, aching discomfort radiating into the mid-palm.
Functional Status: Highly impaired dominant hand function; unable to grasp large objects or perform basic self-care due to the locked, painful digit in the palm.
Social/Safety Concerns: High distress and fear of permanent tendon damage.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 136/84 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, visibly anxious, holding the right hand tightly against the chest, refusing to let anyone touch the locked finger.
Physical Exam: Inspection of the right hand reveals the ring finger rigidly locked in full flexion at the PIP and DIP joints. Crucially, there is a complete absence of true intra-articular joint swelling, intense erythema, or spreading cellulitis. Palpation of the volar palm reveals a distinct, focal, very tender nodule directly over the A1 pulley at the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joint of the ring finger. With gentle reassurance and careful stabilization of the MCP joint, forceful passive manipulation is applied; this achieves full extension, accompanied by a sudden, intensely painful, palpable "triggering" (snapping) release. Following the release, the finger can be actively moved but remains deeply achy.
Environmental Observations: N/A
Medication Reconciliation: No medications taken.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute locked Trigger Finger (Stenosing Tenosynovitis of the A1 Pulley) - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Dupuytren's Contracture - Ruled out by the sudden, acute locking mechanism, the painful snapping release (Dupuytren's is a painless, fixed, non-snapping contracture), and the focal nodule at the A1 pulley rather than a thick palmar cord.

Infectious Flexor Tenosynovitis - Ruled out by the absence of Kanavel's cardinal signs (no symmetrical sausage swelling of the entire digit, no extreme pain along the entire tendon sheath, no flexed resting posture of all digits, and no systemic fever).

Tendon Rupture - Ruled out by the ability to actively flex and extend the digit once the mechanical block was manually bypassed.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a textbook, severe manifestation of stenosing tenosynovitis (trigger finger), presenting as an acutely and rigidly locked digit. The key clinical task was differentiating this mechanical tendon entrapment from an acute tendon rupture or a limb-threatening surgical infection. Physical examination definitively isolated the pathology to a swollen tendon nodule catching on the A1 pulley. The locked position rendered the dominant hand useless and caused immense psychological distress. Postponing evaluation would have left the patient in severe pain and anxiety. Urgent after-hours assessment safely ruled out surgical emergencies, provided the necessary professional manual release of the locked digit, and initiated mechanical splinting to prevent immediate re-locking.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Naproxen 500mg BD with food for 5-7 days to reduce the acute tenosynovial inflammation. Successfully performed a manual passive release of the locked digit on-site.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Wear the provided rigid aluminum finger splint (keeping the knuckle straight) strictly every night while sleeping. Consequence: The splint physically prevents the finger from curling tightly into a fist while asleep, entirely avoiding the terrifying, agonizing locking event that occurs every morning (negative reinforcement).
Action: Soak the hand in a bowl of warm water for 10 minutes every morning before removing the splint. Consequence: Increased local blood flow softens the swollen tendon nodule, making the first active movements of the day smooth and entirely pain-free (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a Hand Therapist or regular GP for consideration of a highly effective localized corticosteroid injection.
DME/Safety (Optional): Fitted a simple aluminum volar splint holding the MCP joint in extension.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1-2 weeks. An ultrasound-guided steroid injection into the tendon sheath is the definitive non-surgical treatment if splinting fails.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the entire finger suddenly swells up like a sausage, becomes bright red and hot to the touch, or if a high fever develops (signs of tendon sheath infection).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a clinical assessment of mechanical tendon entrapment. The manual release is temporary; the swelling remains. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The finger is unlocked, but the tendon remains inflamed and will immediately lock again tonight if not splinted. A steroid injection by your regular doctor is usually the fastest way to permanently shrink the nodule.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if your whole finger suddenly swells massively, becomes bright red and hot, or if you develop a fever and the shivers.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The tendon that bends your finger has developed a swollen nodule (like a knot in a rope). This knot was physically stuck behind a tight tunnel (the A1 pulley) in your palm, locking the finger shut. I have forcefully pulled the knot back through the tunnel, but the swelling is still there. The splint is mandatory tonight to prevent the finger from bending and getting stuck again while you sleep.
Actionable Steps: Do not forcefully squeeze or grip objects today. You must wear the splint tonight. Soak the hand in warm water tomorrow morning before attempting to bend the finger.

